2026-10-24T10:30:32.4790112Z    image gate: kinzie_house_1812 chicago_magazine_1857: verdict "differs", 1 difference(s) open: walls: the engraving draws them light and smooth-faced with a corner board, as weatherboard or whitewash reads, about 1833. The 1812 house is built of squared logs from Moses and Kirkland's text, and Lossing's 1812 view draws them dark and coursed; the plate is evidence of its own date, about twenty years later, and a cladding put on in the 1810s or 1820s is the likeliest reading. Not built for 1812
2026-10-24T10:30:32.4794763Z    image gate: kinzie_house_1812 cram_1893: verdict "differs", 2 difference(s) open: walls: drawn light and smooth, with a corner board at the west end, as weatherboard or whitewash reads, 'in 1832' as the caption says. The same reading as the 1857 engraving's, and the same ruling: the 1812 house is built of squared logs (Moses and Kirkland) as Lossing's 1812 view draws it, and the plate is evidence of its own date, twenty years later. Not built for 1812; piazza height: on the rebuilt piazza (2.95 m plate, Sarony's reading) every post's foot lands 3-7 px above the drawn foot and every head 3-6 px below the drawn head, so the plate still draws the piazza about 1.2 times as tall as the build (1.7 times before T-2516). Sarony's lithograph, drawn by a member of the household, governs the height
2026-10-24T10:30:32.4799139Z    image gate: kinzie_house_1812 eastman_1820: verdict "differs", 1 difference(s) open: walls: drawn smooth and light on the gable end, as weatherboard reads, in 1820; the build's are squared log. Eight years after the 1812 scene and the earliest picture of the house; the same reading as the 1857, Cram and Teich records, and the same ruling: the 1812 house is built of squared logs (Moses and Kirkland) and Lossing's 1812 view draws them coursed
2026-10-24T10:30:32.4802461Z    image gate: kinzie_house_1812 gemmell_1820: verdict "differs", 1 difference(s) open: length: the plate draws the house from its east gable to the end of its north eave over about 62 px (x 1087-1150), the build's runs to x 1167 from this camera, and the Teich painting and Sarony's lithograph also draw the front shorter against the end. Sarony's stacks land within 3 px on a 40 ft ridge, so the roof he drew is the build's length; at 40 px on a blurred scan this plate cannot overrule it, and the 40 ft length stands as its liberty records
